Headwind is the wind component along the runway, so it uses cosine. Crosswind is the sideways component, which X10 already calculates with sine. This formula copies X10's structure (including `ABS` and the `DEGREES` wrapper) and swaps `SIN` for `COS`.

Two things to check. Excel's trig functions expect radians, so converting the angle should normally use `RADIANS`, not `DEGREES`. X10 likely has the same issue. Also, `ABS` hides tailwinds by showing them as positive headwind.

{"formula": "=ABS(X8*COS(DEGREES(U7-X7)))"}

The worksheet repeats the same set of calculations for Column C (rows 23–29) and Column D (rows 32–41). In the Column C block, B27 holds `=MAX(IF(B9:C20<B23,B9:C20))`, the largest value below the last entry. B37 is most likely the Column D version of that calculation, using B32 as the last entry in Column D.

