R1 is labelled "Last Interest Payment Date". P2 adds one coupon period to R2 (`=EDATE(R@,12/G@)`), and Q2 checks that result against the COUPNCD date in L2. So R2 most likely works out the last coupon date by hand, as a cross-check on COUPPCD in M2.

The formula counts the full months from the issue date (E2) to the buy date (D2). It rounds that down to a whole number of coupon periods, each 12/G2 months long. It then moves forward from the issue date by that many periods. This works because the maturity dates in F are a whole number of years after issue, so the coupon schedule lines up with the issue date.

{"formula": "=EDATE(E2,INT(DATEDIF(E2,D2,\"m\")/(12/G2))*(12/G2))"}

